course: books
lesson: 89
title: Just So Stories, How the Alphabet Was Made, part 3: «the Alphabet, all complete», and how letters really began
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Tegumai wants to write a whole message: «Shu-ya-las, ya maru», sky-water ending, come to the river. «Las» is a teaser; Taffy remembers that las also means broken, and wishes she had written «shi-las», broken spear, instead of drawing beavers for the Stranger-man. Her broken-spear drawing becomes the sign for la.
explain: For «maru» they draw a shut mouth for the mum-sound, and for the rough rrr, a saw-edge like Daddy's shark-tooth saw, but with only one tooth, because the simpler the signs, the better for everybody. Taffy asks for a little space between words so they will not jostle, and she reads the whole message out sound by sound.
explain: All evening they draw signs in the smoke on the Cave wall and giggle, and Mummy says «Really, Tegumai, you're worse than my Taffy.» Next morning Taffy finds «Ya-las», water is running out, chalked on the water-tank, and she fills it before running to pull her Daddy's left ear.
explain: They draw all the other signs that day. The T is a family group, Tegumai holding hands with Taffy and Teshumai, drawn so often that it becomes a thin line with arms out. They turn the snake round for Z, make a twiddle for E, beavers for B, noses for N, a pike's mouth for G and K, and the winding river for W, and there is the Alphabet, all complete.
explain: The closing poem is sad and tender: none of the Tribe remain, but each spring Taffy seems to come dancing through the fern on Merrow Down, and far behind comes Tegumai, looking for «the daughter that was all to him».
fact: «Shu-ya-las, ya maru» means «Sky-water ending. River come to», or «the rain is stopping, come down to the river».
fact: «Las» means ending, breaking or finishing; «to-las» means no water in the tank for Mummy, and «shi-las» means the spear is broken.
fact: Taffy: «If I'd only thought of that instead of drawing silly beaver pictures for the Stranger!» Her broken-spear sign (14.) becomes «la» (15.).
fact: «Mum shuts one's mouth up», so a shut mouth is the m-sign (16.); with the open carp-mouth after it, it makes «ma».
fact: The rrr-sound is «all rough and edgy, like your shark-tooth saw»; Tegumai draws one tooth only: «the easier we make our sound-pictures the better for everybody».
fact: Taffy asks for «a little bit of stick or something between each word, so's they won't rub up against each other and jostle»; Daddy says he will leave a space.
fact: «Ya-las», water is ending, is chalked on the big stone water-tank; Taffy fills it from the spring with a bark bucket, then pulls Daddy's left ear, «the one that belonged to her to pull when she was good».
fact: T: Taffy, Tegumai and Teshumai all begin with that sound, so they drew a family holding hands, which became «a thin long Tegumai with his arms out».
fact: Other signs: the snake turned the other way round for Z, «a twiddle for E» (it came into the pictures so often that it got smaller and smaller), the sacred Beaver for B, noses for N, the big lake-pike's mouth for G, the pike's mouth with a spear behind it for K, and the winding Wagai river for W.
fact: Kipling says that after «Hieroglyphics and Demotics ... and Runics ... and all sorts of other ricks and tricks» the alphabet «got back into its proper shape again»; Egyptian hieroglyphs and the runes of northern Europe are real old scripts.
fact: Real life: the alphabet was not made in one day by one family; it grew over about 2,000 years. The Phoenicians, sea-traders of the eastern Mediterranean, spread a 22-letter alphabet about 3,000 years ago; it had no letters for vowel sounds.
fact: Real life: the Greeks borrowed it and added vowels; the Romans took it from the Greeks and the Etruscans and made the Latin alphabet, which English uses. J, U and W were added to English only in the last thousand years.
fact: Real life: our M probably began as wavy water, B as a house, K as an open hand; G was made by the Romans by adding a little bar to C. Old Greek and Latin were often written with no spaces between words; Irish and English monks helped make spaces normal about 1,300 years ago.
fact: Real life: Kipling's own daughter Josephine, the first «Best Beloved» for whom he told these stories, died of an illness in 1899 when she was six, three years before the book came out; many readers hear his sadness in the closing poem.
fact: Talk in this lesson: «slay» = do brilliantly; «cwtch» (Wales) = a cuddle.
exercise: What message does Tegumai want to write? | Shu-ya-las, ya maru: rain ending, come to the river | Yo: bad water, don't drink, Taffy, or you'll get fever | Shi-las: broken spear | So: dinner is ready
exercise: Besides «ending», what else does «las» mean? | Breaking or finishing | Starting something new | Swimming across the river | Fresh water from the spring
exercise: What does «to-las» mean? | No water in the tank for Mummy | The spear is broken in two, just when Daddy is going hunting | Dinner is ending | The river is rising
exercise: What does Taffy wish she had written for the Stranger-man? | «Shi-las», your spear is broken | «Ya maru», come down to the river and bring the spear | «Yo», bad water | «Shu-ya», rain
exercise: Which drawing becomes the sign for la? | Taffy's drawing of the spear broken | The beavers' heads from the picture-letter | A snake | A pike's mouth
exercise: What sign do they make for the mum-sound in «maru»? | A shut mouth | An open carp-mouth with its feeler | A tall mountain peak | A round full moon
exercise: What does the rrr-sound remind Taffy of? | Daddy's shark-tooth saw cutting a plank for the canoe | A growling bear in the woods behind the Cave on Merrow Down | A beaver | The wind
exercise: How many teeth does Tegumai finally put on the saw-sign? | One | Two teeth | A great many | None at all
exercise: Why does he put so few? | The easier the signs, the better | He is tired | Taffy cannot count teeth higher than two, so more would muddle her | The bark is small
exercise: What does Taffy ask to put between words? | A little bit of stick, or a space | A snake | A carp-tail | A dot of mud
exercise: Why does she want the space? | So the words won't jostle, like carps | So the bark is easier to fold and carry in a mendy-bag | So there is room for pictures | So Mummy can add her own words in between
exercise: Where do Taffy and Tegumai draw signs all evening? | In the smoke on the Cave wall | On the tall drying-poles outside the Cave | On the muddy river bank by the carp-pool | On Mummy's deerskin cloak, when she isn't looking
exercise: What does Mummy say about the giggling pair? | «Really, Tegumai, you're worse than my Taffy» | «Go to bed, both of you, at once, and stop drawing on my nice clean wall» | «Show me what it is» | «Wash your hands»
exercise: What does Taffy find chalked on the water-tank next morning? | «Ya-las», water is running out | «Shu-ya», sky-water, it is raining | «So», dinner is cooking on the fire | «Yo», bad water, do not drink it here
exercise: What does Taffy do about the message? | Fills the tank from the spring | Rubs it out with her beaver-skin hood and goes back to sleep | Asks Mummy | Goes back to bed
exercise: Which ear does Taffy pull, and why? | The left one, which was hers to pull when she was good | The right one, because he had written on Mummy's water-tank without asking her first | Both | Neither
exercise: Why is T drawn as a family holding hands? | Taffy, Tegumai and Teshumai all begin with T | The family loves holding hands when they walk to the river together | T looks like a tree | Mummy asked them to
exercise: What did the T-sign become after many drawings? | A thin Tegumai with his arms out | A beaver | A round family circle with three faces | A spear
exercise: How do they make the Z-sign? | Turn the snake round | Draw two snakes, one on top of the other, hissing together | Draw a zigzag river | Draw a zebra
exercise: Why is E «just a twiddle»? | It came into the pictures so often | It is the hardest sound to say clearly | Taffy was too tired to draw any more | E is the sign of a fish in the river
exercise: What do they draw for N, «a nasty, nosy noise»? | Noses | Fishing nets | Hazel nuts | Long necks
exercise: What do they draw for the «scratchy, hurty» Ka-sound? | The pike's mouth with a spear behind it | A shark's tooth, with all its sharp edges scratched in | A broken egg | A cat
exercise: What do they draw for W? | A bit of the winding Wagai river | A wave | A whale | A wing
test: Ivy: «they made the whole alphabet in like five minutes, slay.» (slay = brilliant) Put it right. | It took an afternoon, an evening and the whole next day | Right, they did it in five minutes before lunch on the first day | It took them years | They never finished it
test: Why does Taffy feel a little sad about «shi-las»? | Two simple signs could have said «broken spear» without all the trouble | She cannot draw a spear | Her Daddy's spear is broken again | She forgot the word
test: «The easier we make our sound-pictures the better.» Why is Tegumai right? | Simple signs are quick to write and hard to mix up | Hard signs are prettier, and people remember pretty things better than plain ones | Nobody likes drawing | Bark is expensive
test: What would happen if the T stayed a full family picture? | Writing would be slow and tiring | Nobody could ever read the T-sound, because families are too hard to recognise | It would mean fish | Taffy would cry
test: Which is TRUE in this part? | Taffy reads a written message and does what it says without being told aloud | Mummy is told the secret at once | They give up on la | The Tribe helps draw
test: Real life: did one family make the alphabet in a day? | No: it grew over about 2,000 years | Yes, a father and daughter made it in Surrey in the Stone Age, just as Kipling tells | Yes, the Romans did it | No, it was made last century
test: Real life: what did the Greeks add to the alphabet they borrowed from the Phoenicians? | Letters for vowel sounds | Pictures of animals for every word | Numbers | Spaces between words
test: Real life: which of our letters may have begun as wavy water? | M | O | B | Z
test: Real life: old Greek and Latin were often written how? | With no spaces between words | With small pictures instead of letters | From the top of the page to the bottom | Round and round in circles, like a snail
test: Looking back: in part 2, what did «yo» mean? | Bad swamp-water | Good clear water from the spring behind the Cave | Yes | Dinner
test: Looking back: in part 1, which sign meant ah? | A carp-fish's open mouth | A round egg | A snake | A carp-tail
test: Looking back: what did the Tribe think Taffy's beavers were? | A pack of bad people | Fish swimming up the Wagai river to the beaver-swamp | Trees | Hills
test: Looking back: in the first Taffy story, the Head Chief foretold how many letters? | Twenty-six | Twenty-two | Ten | Six
test: Tom from Cardiff: «Tegumai gave Taffy a cwtch after the rain.» (cwtch = cuddle) What did Tegumai really do when the rain came? | Got up and danced | Sulked because the rain spoiled his fishing | Went home | Wrote to Mummy
test: Real life: Kipling's poem says «none remain» and Tegumai is far behind, looking for his daughter. What sad real event do many readers think of? | Kipling's daughter Josephine died at six, three years before the book came out | Kipling lost his way on Merrow Down | Kipling's house burned | Kipling's dog ran away
test: ★★★ Careful: «Ya-las» was chalked on the tank. Which signs from the story would spell it, in order? | Carp-tail, carp-mouth, broken-spear la, carp-mouth, snake | Snake, egg, carp-mouth | Shut mouth, carp-mouth, saw | Egg, snake, broken spear
test: ★★★ Taffy, Tegumai, Teshumai all start with T. Which of these Tribe words, also in the story, starts with a sound they had already made a sign for BEFORE the T? | Shu | Tewara | Taffimai | Tegumai
test: ★★★ Real life: the Phoenician alphabet had 22 letters and English has 26. Which three letters did English add only in the last thousand years? | J, U and W | A, B and C | X, Y and Z | E, O and I
exam: Identify the sign adopted by Tegumai for the rough sound r. | A saw-edge reduced to a single tooth | An open carp-mouth with a feeler | A shut mouth | A hissy-snake reversed
exam: Ascertain the content of the message chalked upon the water-tank on the following morning. | Ya-las, the water was ending | Shu-ya, signifying that rain was to be expected before noon | So, signifying dinner | Shi-las, signifying a broken spear
exam: It is given that the T-sound was represented by a family group. Select the response most consistent with the subsequent development of that sign. | It became a thin figure of Tegumai with his arms outstretched | It became the picture of a beaver | It was abandoned in favour of a snake | It became a round egg
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the real historical origin of the alphabet? | It developed over some two thousand years among several peoples, the Greeks adding vowels | It was devised in a single day by a Neolithic father and daughter | It was invented by the Romans without any predecessor | It descended directly from the runes
